@page "/exgrid/selection"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Selection and keyboard"
          Lead="Selection works as it does in Excel: rectangles, a Focus that stays put while an Extent moves, and Enter and Tab that cycle inside what you selected. Selecting is cheap, so it is never capped.">

    <DocsSection Title="Rectangles, a Focus and an Extent">
        <p>
            A selection is one or more rectangles of cells. The <strong>Focus</strong> is Excel's active cell: the
            cell typing goes into, outlined inside the selection. The <strong>Extent</strong> is the corner that
            moves while you extend a range.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Click</strong> selects one cell. <strong>Drag</strong> or <strong>Shift+click</strong> draws a rectangle from the Focus.</li>
            <li><strong>Ctrl+click</strong> adds another rectangle. On a cell that is already selected, it takes that cell out.</li>
            <li>Clicking a <strong>header</strong> sorts. <strong>Shift+click</strong> on a header selects whole columns, and <strong>Ctrl+Space</strong> does from the keyboard.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Positions are in the current order. When the order of the rows changes — a sort, a filter, a row
            inserted — the same positions would name other rows, so the grid <strong>drops the selection</strong>
            rather than leave it on rows you did not choose.
        </p>
        <p>
            Keys typed against the dropped selection that were still on their way when the order changed go with
            it: typing opens no editor and writes nothing, and the grid says so through <code>OnEditDiscarded</code>,
            once for the whole run of keys; an arrow moves nothing. A key pressed once the new order is on screen
            places the Focus on the first cell in view, as the first key on an empty selection always does.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Reading the selection" Builtin="typeof(SelectionStatus)" Mud="typeof(SelectionStatusMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            <code>SelectionChanged</code> hands over a <code>GridSelection</code>: its ranges, the Focus, and the cell
            count. Try Ctrl+click, then Ctrl+Shift+↓.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The keyboard">
        <p>Click into the grid, or tab to it: the grid is one tab stop, and its keys are Excel's.</p>
        @* The rows the Keyboard reference lists for this section, so the two are one list (ADR-0110). *@
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 34%">Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Keyboard.ExplainedAt("exgrid/selection#the-keyboard"))
                {
                    <tr><td><KeyText Text="@row.Keys" /></td><td><KeyText Text="@row.What" /></td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            <strong>Why Enter and Tab never leave a range:</strong> select five cells and type five values, pressing
            Enter after each. The fifth Enter returns to the first cell instead of carrying on into a sixth row you
            did not mean to touch. Excel's "edge of the data block" for Ctrl+arrow is not used: query results have
            no blank rows, and finding a block's edge would need every row.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every key of the three products, each with the page that explains it, is in the
            <a href="reference/keyboard">Keyboard reference</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The page follows the Focus">
        <p>
            A move the grid makes — the arrows, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + arrow, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>,
            <kbd>PageUp</kbd> and <kbd>PageDown</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Tab</kbd>, and
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Backspace</kbd> — first brings the cell into the grid's own Viewport, then asks the
            browser to bring it into the window, at the nearest edge: on a grid taller than the window, the page
            scrolls as the Focus walks past the fold, as a spreadsheet's window does. While a range is extended it
            is the far end that is followed. Opening the editor follows the same rule, so a cell is followed the same
            way whether it is reached or edited. A cell already in the window moves nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nothing is followed once the keyboard has left the grid, or once the Focus has moved on, and a resize, or
            a panel opening beside the grid, never moves the page: only a move does.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A page with a fixed bar sets <code>scroll-padding</code></strong> on its scrolling element, so that
            a cell is not brought in under the bar: the browser places it, and the bar is the page's to know about.
            This site sets <code>html { scroll-padding-top: … }</code> to its app bar's height.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Status-bar figures">
        <p>
            Select two cells or more and the status line under the grid shows what Excel's status bar shows:
            <strong>Average, Count and Sum</strong>. Right-click the line to choose among all six — Average,
            Count, Numerical Count, Min, Max and Sum. The figures cover <strong>every selected cell</strong>, the
            rows not painted and not yet fetched included: Ctrl+Shift+↓ down a column of a million rows sums a
            million rows.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Who sums.</strong> The grid does not hold your data, so it asks, as it does for
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>. <code>GridSource.From</code> answers by itself; a server answers
                through <code>GridSource.Fetch(..., summarize: ...)</code>; a grid you push rows to answers through
                <code>OnSummarize</code>. With nobody to ask, no figure is shown.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>What counts.</strong> A number is in every figure, summed exactly when it is an integer or a
                <code>decimal</code>. Text, a Boolean and a date are counted and never summed. A blank is in none.
                A cell under two ranges counts once.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Never the wrong total.</strong> Change the selection and the figures go at once; they come
                back only as the answer to the selection you now have. A selection the server will not sum shows
                its reason, never part of a total.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>On by default.</strong> Any grid that can summarise shows the line, so that selecting does
                not move the rows under your hand. <code>ShowSelectionSummary="false"</code> takes it off the
                grid; <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code> still hands you the figures, for a status bar of your own.
                Bind <code>SummaryFigures</code> to keep the menu's choice; without a binding the menu is not offered.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="Status-bar figures" Builtin="typeof(SelectionFigures)" Mud="typeof(SelectionFiguresMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            Select some Notional cells, then right-click the line under the grid. <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code>
            hands the same figures to a status bar of your own, shown under the grid.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Which row am I on?">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>HighlightFocusRow</code> paints a band across the row holding the Focus — Excel 365's Focus
                Cell — which helps on a grid wider than the screen.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>HighlightHoverRow</code> paints a band under the pointer, what design systems call
                <em>hover</em>. On a <code>MudExGridPaper</code>, <code>Hover="true"</code> turns it on.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Both are drawn by an overlay, not by the rows, so moving them renders no row. Their colours are the
            Visual Tokens <code>--ex-focus-row-fill</code> and <code>--ex-row-hover-background</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

